## Proof

**Step 1: Recognize the surface as a helicoid.**

Set $r = e^{v} > 0$ and $\theta = e^{u} > 0$. Since $(u,v)\mapsto (r,\theta)=(e^{v},e^{u})$ is a diffeomorphism $\mathbb{R}^{2}\to(0,\infty)^{2}$, the surface is reparametrized as
$$X(r,\theta) = (r\cos\theta,\; r\sin\theta,\; \theta), \qquad r>0,\;\theta>0.$$
This is (a patch of) the **helicoid** with pitch $c=1$.

**Step 2: First and second fundamental forms (light computation).**

$$X_r=(\cos\theta,\sin\theta,0),\quad X_\theta=(-r\sin\theta,r\cos\theta,1).$$
$$E=1,\quad F=0,\quad G=r^{2}+1.$$

The unit normal is $n=\dfrac{X_r\times X_\theta}{|X_r\times X_\theta|}=\dfrac{(\sin\theta,-\cos\theta,r)}{\sqrt{1+r^{2}}}$.

Second derivatives:
$$X_{rr}=0,\quad X_{r\theta}=(-\sin\theta,\cos\theta,0),\quad X_{\theta\theta}=(-r\cos\theta,-r\sin\theta,0).$$

Hence
$$L=n\cdot X_{rr}=0,\quad M=n\cdot X_{r\theta}=\frac{-1}{\sqrt{1+r^{2}}},\quad N=n\cdot X_{\theta\theta}=0.$$

**Step 3: Principal curvatures.**

Mean curvature:
$$H=\frac{EN-2FM+GL}{2(EG-F^{2})}=\frac{1\cdot0-0+(r^{2}+1)\cdot0}{2(r^{2}+1)}=0.$$

Gaussian curvature:
$$K=\frac{LN-M^{2}}{EG-F^{2}}=\frac{-M^{2}}{r^{2}+1}=-\frac{1}{(1+r^{2})^{2}}.$$

Since $H=0$ and $K<0$, the principal curvatures are
$$\kappa_{1,2}=H\pm\sqrt{H^{2}-K}=\pm\frac{1}{\sqrt{-K}}\cdot\sqrt{-K}\;=\;\pm\frac{1}{1+r^{2}}.$$

Substituting back $r=e^{v}$:

$$\boxed{\kappa_{1}=\frac{1}{1+e^{2v}},\qquad \kappa_{2}=-\frac{1}{1+e^{2v}}.}$$

(The helicoid is a minimal surface, so $\kappa_{1}=-\kappa_{2}$, confirming $H=0$.)

**Step 4: Asymptotic lines.**

The asymptotic-line equation is $L\,dr^{2}+2M\,dr\,d\theta+N\,d\theta^{2}=0$. With $L=N=0$ and $M\neq0$:
$$2M\,dr\,d\theta=0\;\Longrightarrow\;dr\,d\theta=0.$$

Thus either $dr=0$ ($r=\text{const}$, i.e. $v=\text{const}$) or $d\theta=0$ ($\theta=\text{const}$, i.e. $u=\text{const}$).

$$\boxed{\text{The asymptotic lines are the coordinate curves }u=\text{const}\text{ and }v=\text{const}.}$$

Geometrically: $v=\text{const}$ gives the **helices** $\theta\mapsto(r_{0}\cos\theta,r_{0}\sin\theta,\theta)$, and $u=\text{const}$ gives the **rulings** (straight lines) $r\mapsto(r\cos\theta_{0},r\sin\theta_{0},\theta_{0})$. The rulings are straight lines (zero normal curvature), and since $H=0$ forces $\kappa_{1}=-\kappa_{2}$, the orthogonal helices are automatically the conjugate asymptotic family.

### PROOF COMPLETE
